---
<Docs
  title="Levni CLI reference: render, animate, storyboard, brand, board, poster, list, mcp"
  description="Every levni command with its arguments, flags and examples: render scenes, animate them as GIF, WebP or MP4, make storyboards, brand kits, boards and posters, search and import drawings, run the MCP server."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Use Levni</p>
  <h1>Command line</h1>
  <p class="lede">The <code>levni</code> command draws scenes and animations, searches and checks the library, and makes boards, posters, storyboards and brand kits. Everything is deterministic: the same input gives the same file.</p>

  <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
  <p>After <code>npm install @levni/levni</code>, run commands with <code>npx @levni/levni …</code>. From a checkout, use <code>node bin/levni.mjs …</code>. Running <code>levni</code> with no command, or with <code>help</code>, prints the usage text. An unknown command prints it and exits with code 1.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Commands, scrolls sideways"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{rows.map(([c, d]) => <tr><td><code>{c}</code></td><td>{d}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Errors print as <code>levni: message</code> and exit with code 1.</p>

  <h2 id="render">render</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni render &lt;scene.json&gt; &lt;out.png|out.webp|out.svg&gt; [--preview] [--format cover|og|square|story|youtube|hd|readme]
              [--transparent] [--tile 512]</code></pre>
  <p>Draws a scene and prints the output path, then the layout critic’s notes (or <code>critic: no layout problems found</code>).</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>--preview</code> also writes <code>&lt;out&gt;.prev.jpg</code>, 560 px wide.</li>
    <li><code>--format</code> recomposes a 1200 by 673 scene for another canvas by setting <code>format</code> and <code>reflow</code> on the scene. <code>hd</code> is 1920 by 1080 and <code>readme</code> is 1280 by 640. See <a href="/docs/scenes/#formats-and-reflow">Formats and reflow</a>.</li>
    <li><code>--transparent</code> draws on a clear ground with the soft finish and no grain (a PNG keeps the alpha). See <a href="/docs/export/#transparent">Export extras</a>.</li>
    <li><code>--tile N</code> writes a seamless N px square texture as a PNG (default 512). It suits mesh gradients, dither and grain, not pictures with objects. See <a href="/docs/export/#tilecheck-and-tileable">Export extras</a>.</li>
    <li>A <code>.webp</code> output is compressed under a size budget; <code>.svg</code> writes the vector source.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">npx @levni/levni render scene.json cover.webp
npx @levni/levni render scene.json og.png --format og
npx @levni/levni render scene.json out.png --preview
npx @levni/levni render scene.json sticker.png --transparent
npx @levni/levni render scene.json paper.png --tile 512</code></pre>

  <h2 id="animate">animate</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni animate &lt;scene.json&gt; &lt;out.gif|out.webp|out.mp4&gt; [--frames 24] [--fps 12] [--boil 3] [--width 800]
              [--colours 128] [--format og|square|story|youtube|hd|readme] [--strip frames.png]
              [--frames-dir dir] [--sprite sheet.png --columns 6] [--crf 20 --loops 1]</code></pre>
  <p>Draws a scene as a loop: the scene’s <code>t</code> runs from 0 to 1 over the frames. Elements move on their own (clouds, trees, flags, globes) or through <code>motion</code>, <code>stagger</code> and <code>camera</code>. Prints the file, frame count, size and the critic’s notes for the first frame. Needs Levni 0.5.0.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>--frames</code>, <code>--fps</code>, <code>--width</code>, <code>--colours</code>: length, speed, output width and GIF palette size.</li>
    <li><code>--boil N</code>: N crayon textures take turns, for a hand-drawn shimmer.</li>
    <li><code>--strip</code> also writes a few frames side by side so you can look at the loop.</li>
    <li><code>.mp4</code> output needs <code>ffmpeg</code> on the PATH; <code>--crf</code> and <code>--loops</code> apply to MP4 only.</li>
    <li><code>--frames-dir</code> also writes <code>frame-0001.png</code> and so on, with <code>frames.json</code>. <code>--sprite</code> also writes a sprite sheet and its atlas JSON; <code>--columns</code> sets the grid.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">npx @levni/levni animate scene.json loop.gif --width 800
npx @levni/levni animate scene.json loop.webp --frames 36 --fps 12 --boil 3 --strip frames.png
npx @levni/levni animate scene.json loop.mp4
npx @levni/levni animate scene.json loop.gif --frames-dir frames --sprite sheet.png --columns 6</code></pre>
  <p>Full guide: <a href="/docs/animation/">Animation</a>. Frames, sprite sheets and MP4: <a href="/docs/export/">Export extras</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="sheet">sheet</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni sheet &lt;out.png&gt; [prop ...] [--recolor coral=blue,mustard=tone]</code></pre>
  <p>A contact sheet of drawings: every variant, on paper and on a blue panel, labelled <code>name number</code>. With no names it draws the whole library (a long sheet). <code>--recolor</code> applies a colour swap to every drawing, using the house colour names.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">npx @levni/levni sheet look.png rocket lighthouse dove
npx @levni/levni sheet recolour.png rocket --recolor coral=blue</code></pre>
  <p>Prints how many variants it drew. The result looks like the sheet in <a href="/docs/getting-started/#first-render">Getting started</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="list">list</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni list [query]</code></pre>
  <p>Lists drawings as <code>name  (variants)  tags</code>. With a query, only those whose name, old name or tags contain it (case-insensitive, English or Turkish). Ends with a count.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-text">$ npx @levni/levni list lighthouse
building-lighthouse  (1)  building lighthouse
lighthouse  (3)  deniz feneri, fener, lighthouse, beacon, coast, kıyı, sea, deniz, istanbul
2 props</code></pre>

  <h2 id="check">check</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni check [prop ...]</code></pre>
  <p>For agents that cannot see images. Validates drawings and measures each variant: how much of the 0 to 100 box it fills, its centre, its colours, and what to fix. With names it prints a line for each variant, plus <code>note:</code> lines where the name, tags or notes break the <a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/folio-guide.md`}>Folio guide</a>; with none it checks the whole library and prints only problems, ending with <code>all ok</code> or <code>N thing(s) to fix</code>.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-text">$ npx @levni/levni check rocket
rocket#0  size 83  centre (46,53)  colours tone-coral,paper,ink,tone,coral,mustard  ok
rocket#1  size 77  centre (51,44)  colours paper,ink,tone-coral,tone,coral,mustard  ok
all ok</code></pre>

  <h2 id="add">add</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni add &lt;file&gt; [--dir &lt;props folder&gt;] [--sheet out.png]</code></pre>
  <p>Imports drawings written elsewhere. The file can hold one drawing, an array, or a chat answer with <code>```json</code> blocks. Each drawing is validated and test-drawn. New drawings are written as <code>&lt;name&gt;.json</code>; a drawing that already exists gains its new variants; broken ones are skipped with the reason, and the command exits 1 if any were skipped. It also prints <code>note</code> lines for style hints that do not block a drawing: a tag that repeats the name, fewer than three tags, a number in the name that the drawing does not show, a note ending in a full stop.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-text">note book-2: name "book-2" ends in a number the drawing does not show: name it for what makes it different (book-open, not book-2)
new  book-2: 1 variant(s)</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><code>--dir</code> chooses the folder. The default is the library of the checkout you run it from.</li>
    <li><code>--sheet</code> draws the imported drawings so you can look at them.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">npx @levni/levni add my-props.json --sheet check.png</code></pre>
  <p>The drawing format and the review rules are in <a href="/docs/library/#submit">the library guide</a>; naming and tagging in <a href="/docs/library/#names">names, tags and notes</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="board">board</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni board &lt;spec.json&gt; &lt;out.png&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>Makes a brand board. The shortest spec is <code>{`{ "brand": { name, tagline, mark, inks } }`}</code>, which gives nine tiles; or place scene, palette and type tiles yourself, with browser, card, tote and phone mockups. Prints the size and file weight. Full format: <a href="/docs/boards/">Brand boards</a>.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">npx @levni/levni board board.json board.png</code></pre>

  <h2 id="poster">poster</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni poster &lt;spec.json&gt; &lt;out-dir&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>Makes a series of 1200 by 1600 posters from one brand: <code>poster-01-split.png</code> to <code>poster-08-launch.png</code> and <code>series.png</code>. Full format: <a href="/docs/posters/">Branding posters</a>.</p>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">npx @levni/levni poster brand.json posters/</code></pre>

  <h2 id="storyboard">storyboard</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni storyboard &lt;spec.json&gt; &lt;out.png&gt; [--frames-dir dir] [--columns n]</code></pre>
  <p>Draws a shot list as one contact sheet: a numbered frame for every shot with its title, length, running timecode and note. A bare <code>{'[{ id, desc, frames }]'}</code> list works as a spec. Prints the size, the shot count and the total seconds, then any warnings. Needs Levni 0.5.0.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>--frames-dir</code> also writes <code>shot-01.png</code> and so on at the full format size, and <code>shots.json</code> with each shot’s start time.</li>
    <li><code>--columns</code> sets frames per row.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">npx @levni/levni storyboard storyboard.json story.png --frames-dir frames</code></pre>
  <p>Full format: <a href="/docs/storyboards/">Storyboards</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="brand">brand</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni brand &lt;brand.json | https://url&gt; &lt;out-dir&gt; [--posters 8] [--only mark,og,loop]</code></pre>
  <p>Makes a brand kit in one folder: mark, favicons, palette, social cards, story, README banner, board, posters, a loop, <code>brand.json</code>, <code>kit.json</code> and <code>index.html</code>. The first argument is a spec file or a website address; an address is fetched once to read the name, tagline and colours, and nothing else is fetched. Prints the file count, the brand and the time, then the critic’s notes per asset. Needs Levni 0.5.0.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>--posters 8</code> renders every poster template; the default is four.</li>
    <li><code>--only</code> makes only the named assets, separated by commas.</li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">npx @levni/levni brand brand.json kit/
npx @levni/levni brand brand.json kit/ --only mark,og,loop</code></pre>
  <p>Full format: <a href="/docs/brand-kits/">Brand kits</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="mcp">mcp</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni mcp</code></pre>
  <p>Runs Levni as an MCP server over stdio. You normally do not run it yourself; your client does. See <a href="/docs/mcp/">MCP server</a> for client setup and every tool.</p>

  <h2 id="ghost">ghost</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni ghost drafts [--dir &lt;folder&gt;] [--apply]
levni ghost attach &lt;plan.json&gt; [--apply]</code></pre>
  <p>The Ghost adapter: it finds drafts that have no images and attaches a cover and inline images. It is a dry run unless you pass <code>--apply</code>, only touches drafts, never publishes or sends a newsletter, and skips drafts that already have images. Needs <code>GHOST_ADMIN_API_URL</code> and <code>GHOST_ADMIN_API_KEY</code> in the environment (Ghost Admin, Settings, Integrations, a custom integration); without them it prints usage and exits 1.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>drafts</code> lists imageless drafts; with <code>--apply</code> it also pulls each one to <code>./levni-drafts/&lt;name&gt;/</code> as <code>article.md</code> and <code>meta.json</code>.</li>
    <li><code>attach</code> reads a plan file naming each draft, its cover scene and the inline images with the heading each goes after.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Details and the plan file format: <a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/ghost.md`}>docs/ghost.md</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="judge">judge</h2>
  <pre><code class="language-bash">levni judge score &lt;image&gt; --brief "..." [--model &lt;id&gt;]
levni judge pick &lt;image&gt; &lt;image&gt; [...] --brief "..." [--model &lt;id&gt;]</code></pre>
  <p>Optional. Asks a judge model on OpenRouter to rate images from 0 to 3 for a brief and prints JSON. <code>pick</code> scores every image twice in shuffled order and averages; gaps under 0.15 count as ties. Needs your own <code>OPENROUTER_API_KEY</code>. Details: <a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/judge.md`}>docs/judge.md</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="env">Environment variables</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Environment variables, scrolls sideways"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Variable</th><th>Used by</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>LEVNI_RASTER</code></td><td>render, board, poster, sheet, mcp</td><td><code>rsvg</code> hands the crayon finish back to the SVG renderer (slower, about 1 s a cover instead of 0.15 s). Leave unset.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>LEVNI_PROPS</code></td><td>all</td><td>Extra folders of drawings, separated by <code>:</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>LEVNI_ICONS</code></td><td><code>icon</code>, <code>ascii</code></td><td>Path to Tabler Icons, if you did not install <code>@tabler/icons</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GHOST_ADMIN_API_URL</code>, <code>GHOST_ADMIN_API_KEY</code></td><td><code>ghost</code>, MCP</td><td>Your Ghost Admin credentials. Never read from files.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>OPENROUTER_API_KEY</code></td><td><code>judge</code></td><td>Your own OpenRouter key.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
</Docs>
